AB  - Speaking anxiety is a persistent challenge for many learners of English as a foreign language, particularly those with limited prior experience in real communicative settings. Virtual Reality (VR) has emerged as a promising tool for providing immersive, low-pressure speaking practice, while recent advances in artificial intelligence enable responsive dialogue support through speech-to-text, large language models, and text-to-speech synthesis. However, most VR systems remain single-user, limiting opportunities for peer interaction that may influence affective outcomes. This study compares two learning modalities: an AIsupported single-user VR environment and an AI-supported Collaborative VR (CVR) environment in which pairs of learners interact jointly during task-based speaking activities. A total of 20 undergraduate students were assigned to either the VR group (n = 10) or the CVR group (n = 10). Both groups completed three scenario-based speaking tasks and responded to pre- and post-test questionnaires measuring competence, autonomy, relatedness, and motivation, based on Self-Determination Theory. Independent-samples t-tests were used to compare group outcomes. Results showed no significant differences between groups at pre-test, indicating comparable baseline levels. Post-test analysis revealed that the CVR group achieved significantly higher relatedness scores, suggesting that collaborative interaction enhanced learners' sense of social connection. No significant differences were found for competence, autonomy, or motivation. The findings highlight the potential benefits of integrating AI-driven conversational support with collaborative VR design. Implications for future VR-based language learning systems are discussed, particularly for supporting low-proficiency learners.
ER  - 

