AB  - This paper develops a digital special effects generation model based on a generative adversarial network (GAN), aiming to improve the realism and speed of special effects generation in VR scenes. First, a feature encoder is constructed, which takes user actions and environmental parameters as input and maps the captured multidimensional data into a latent space vector. The generator in the GAN generates high-resolution special effects images based on this vector. The discriminator evaluates the realism of the special effects and their consistency with the environment, jointly optimizing and improving the output quality. Techniques such as data augmentation and frame-by-frame supervision are employed during training to ensure the model's generalization and real-time performance in complex scenarios. Experimental results show that this method achieves an average special effects generation latency of $17.36$ milliseconds per frame on an Oculus Quest 2 device, improves the special effects detail score by $23.3\%$ compared to traditional algorithms, and achieves an average user immersion score of 8.91.
ER  - 

