AB  - In software development, requirements analysis is critical to project success. The Problem Framework (PF) is a mainstream requirements analysis method that uses Problem Diagrams (PD) to achieve more precise, consistent, and verifiable modeling of software problems. However, traditional 2D problem diagrams have limited interactivity and static layouts, which hinder efficient understanding. This paper introduces ME4PD, an extended prototype of our previous system XR4PD. XR4PD enables immersive 3D visualization of problem diagrams in an augmented reality (AR) environment. ME4PD introduces a multimodal interface supporting natural language and voice-driven requirement modeling. Through a dynamic integration mechanism, it enables real-time expansion of domain-requirement-phenomenon triplets generated by large language models. To ensure structural consistency, the system incorporates a semantic similarity module using word vector embeddings and cosine similarity, enabling intelligent alignment between newly generated nodes and existing graph entities. Matching nodes are automatically reused and connected, while novel concepts are seamlessly added and integrated into the graph layout. Feedback from the conducted assessment was positive.
ER  - 

