AB  - We present an augmented reality (AR) caregiver system on the Microsoft HoloLens 2 that assists elderly users with medication management tasks. The system provides AR guidance for pill identification, dosage reminders, and step-by-step instructions, controlled via voice commands and hand gestures. The platform integrates on-device edge computing with AI modules, including a TinyBERT-based natural language processing (NLP) model and computer vision components. To achieve low-latency, low-power performance, we employ neuromorphic-inspired optimizations-event-driven architecture, memory locality, parallel task pipelines, and adaptive frame-skipping. This design enables real-time, offline AI inference without cloud dependency. Empirical results demonstrate performance under the HoloLens 2's limited power budget. Our contributions include enabling fully offline AI inference on a wearable AR device, achieving real-time multimodal guidance, and introducing a neuromorphic-inspired architecture that improves energy efficiency for AR caregiving applications.
