AB  - Virtual reality (VR) provides an immersive platform for human-human and human-AI communication. In social and professional VR settings, intelligent virtual agents (IVAs), powered by large language models (LLMs), have great potential to facilitate these communications. In this context, non-verbal communication including expressing emotions is crucial to social communication. In this paper, we developed two IVAs that are identical in appearance but diverse in their emotional expression capabilities. Both were capable of expressing six basic emotions (happiness, sadness, fear, anger, surprise, and disgust) but one of them showed these emotions with a granular intensity level. We conducted a user study with 30 participants to examine whether this difference in expressing emotions affects their perception of the IVAs. Participants held interactive conversations with both IVAs but did not perceive them as significantly different. Based on our quantitative and qualitative results, we discuss insights for non-verbal communication with IVAs.
