AB  - Synthesizing realistic and anatomically consistent B-mode ultrasound (US) images under arbitrary ultrasound probe (US-probe) motion remains a challenging problem due to the conflicting requirements of pose-dependent anatomical alignment and stochastic speckle texture modeling. Generative models must preserve structural consistency governed by six-degree-of-freedom (6-DoF) US-probe poses while simultaneously reproducing realistic ultrasound speckle statistics. This study investigates architectural decomposition strategies for pose-conditioned ultrasound image synthesis. We propose a Three-Generator conditional GAN (CGAN) that explicitly separates translation-aware anatomical layout, rotation-dependent structural refinement, and speckle-specific texture generation into dedicated generator branches. To evaluate the effectiveness of this design, we conduct extensive comparisons against Single- and Two-Generator CGAN variants, as well as a conditional diffusion baseline, evaluated under a unified evaluation protocol. Model performance is assessed using a comprehensive evaluation protocol covering standard image fidelity metrics, ultrasound-specific realism measures, anatomy-preservation metrics, pose–image alignment, and efficiency of computation. Experimental results on a phantom dataset demonstrate that architectural decomposition consistently improves anatomical fidelity, speckle realism, and pose consistency. In particular, the proposed Three-Generator pose-conditioned CGAN achieves the best overall performance across all evaluation criteria, reaching SSIM = 0.903, PSNR = 27.982 dB, and FID = 1.810 while maintaining stable and efficient inference. The results indicate that explicit architectural separation provides an effective framework for high-fidelity, pose-aware ultrasound image synthesis.
ER  - 

